An `@` pattern the shell stripped of its quotes is refused with the quoted form to type, since no
file is named `a.ts@cap`. The second call is the control: typed as suggested, the same pattern
resolves.

```console
$ lets show a.ts@cap
? 1
a.ts@cap: no such file · did you mean "a.ts@'cap'"
ERROR_CODE=not_found

$ lets show "a.ts@'cap'"
── a.ts@'cap'  (1-1 of 1)
1 	export const cap = 10;
── showed 1 target · 1 line

```
